Title:
Hunt-Sleeping-Beacons

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- Hunt-Sleeping-Beacons is a Windows userland callstack scanner intended to spot indicators of “sleeping” C2 beacons by analyzing why a thread is idling between callbacks.  
- It looks for artifacts consistent with unpacked/injected agents and common sleepmask implementations, combining callstack inspection with memory and threadpool/timer introspection.  
- Checks include unbacked RX/RWX memory in stacks, non-executable pages (permission-flip sleepmasks), module stomping via working set attributes, and suspicious APC/timer-based sleep patterns.  
- A notable feature is enumerating threadpool timers and extracting their callback addresses from userland using `NtQueryInformationWorkerFactory` and internal threadpool structures to flag callbacks pointing to suspicious APIs (e.g., `ntdll!NtContinue`).  
- It also flags abnormal intermodular call sequences (ntdll→kernel32→ntdll) associated with module proxying and patterns suggestive of return-address spoofing.  
- Results are heuristic (false positives expected, especially module stomping); .NET and 32-bit processes are ignored by default.  
- Useful primarily for Blue Team/DFIR and threat hunters, and for Red Teams to understand detectable artifacts of sleepmask/callstack-evasion tradecraft.

Technical Focus:
- Callstack-based IOC hunting for idle/sleeping threads
- Memory region attribution (private/unbacked, RX/RWX, NX transitions)
- Module stomping detection via `MEMORY_WORKING_SET_EX_INFORMATION` / `SharedOriginal`
- APC- and timer-based sleepmask detection (`KiUserApcDispatcher`, `RtlpTpTimerCallback`, `NtContinue`)
- Userland enumeration of threadpool timers via `NtQueryInformationWorkerFactory` and `WORKER_FACTORY_BASIC_INFORMATION`
- Detection heuristics for module proxying and return-address spoofing patterns

Use Cases:
- Hunt for injected/unpacked C2 agents in long-running processes
- Detect sleepmask implementations that rely on APCs or threadpool timers
- Identify potential module-stomped images using working set metadata
- Triage suspicious processes by correlating blocking callstacks with memory/timer artifacts
- Validate/measure detectability of callstack-evasion techniques (module proxying, return spoofing)

Keywords:
Windows, callstack scanning, sleeping beacons, C2 beacon, injection, unpacking, unbacked memory, RWX, NX, module stomping, copy-on-write, MEMORY_WORKING_SET_EX_INFORMATION, VirtualAttributes.SharedOriginal, APC, KiUserApcDispatcher, NtContinue, threadpool, timer queue, RtlpTpTimerCallback, NtQueryInformationWorkerFactory, WORKER_FACTORY_BASIC_INFORMATION, PoolParty, module proxying, return address spoofing